artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a three-dimensional two-component Fermi gas consisting of "spin-up" fermions of mass m↑ and "spin-down" fermions of mass m↓, interacting only via ↑↓ s-wave interactions tuned near a magnetic Feshbach resonance. Assume the mass ratio m↑/m↓ is sufficiently large that the Efimov effect occurs in the three-body (and more generally N-body) sector with two ↑ fermions and one ↓ fermion (and their natural N-body extensions): there exist Efimovian N-body bound states (N ≥ 3) whose spectrum, in the strict zero-range limit, is unbounded from below (Thomas collapse) and whose three-body recombination rate diverges. Now model the interaction by a narrow Feshbach resonance, characterized by a finite, negative effective range r_e (equivalently, a microscopic length b → 0 limit in which the effective range remains nonzero), so that:
(i) the N-body spectrum becomes bounded from below;
(ii) the three-body loss rate tends to zero in the relevant limit;
(iii) the Efimovian discrete-scaling bound-state structure persists at moderate energies.

Question: Determine whether, under these conditions, long-lived Efimovian N-body bound states can be prepared and experimentally observed (i.e., have lifetimes much longer than typical experimental and trap timescales) in such a cold-atom Fermi gas with a sufficiently large mass ratio and a narrow Feshbach resonance providing a stabilizing nonzero effective range. Equivalently, exhibit a concrete realization (atomic species, mass ratio, resonance parameters) and demonstrate—theoretically and experimentally—the stable preparation and observation of these Efimovian N-body states.

